  "alpha_phi_identification": "alpha(phi) := Psi_phi, the scalar self-energy lensing potential solving nabla^2 Psi_phi = 2 kappa_phi in the thin-lens limit. This is the first evaluation of the manuscript's Eq. 3_methodology_02a kernel with a defined alpha.",
  "verdict": "Channel A (strict Einstein-frame scalar self-energy): at the Refsdal image sheet densities (g/g_t = 4.1-5.9, y = 0.27-0.34), the canonical screening profile gives |grad u| = yg/c^2 ~ 6.15e-27 m^-1 at SX, i.e. rho_phi ~ 6.36e-27 kg/m^3. Projected over a fiducial 1.0 Mpc halo depth this is kappa_phi ~ 3.96e-05, a SX-S1 differential Fermat delay of 21241.407 s (0.24585 d) versus the observed +30.0 d \u2014 a shortfall of ~2e+03. Channel B (treating the scalar fifth force as an equivalent lensing density, kappa_ph = 2 beta^2 y kappa) would give +3778.63 d, overshooting the residual \u2014 but the fifth force acts on matter geodesics of the matter metric, not on g's curvature; photons couple only to T_mn^phi (channel A). Verdict: the direct backreaction channel cannot supply the observed amplitude at canonical parameters; the +30.1 d residual, if TEP-sourced, must enter through the mass-sheet/dynamics-slip systematics channel (step_60), not through scalar curvature of the light path. This converts the log-magnification proxy's open kernel question into a derived bound: alpha(phi) = Psi_phi is now computed, and it is ~1e-6."
